# Attributes the work to the company rather than to an individual.
#
# git log, git shortlog and git blame read this and substitute the name on the left for any commit
# authored by an identity on the right, so past commits present under one umbrella without rewriting
# a single SHA. That matters here in particular: one early commit was authored from an employer
# address, and removing it for real would mean a force push that changes every commit id.
#
# Note what this does not do — GitHub's web UI ignores .mailmap and still shows the original author
# on a commit page. Only a history rewrite changes that, which is a separate decision.
